# EVIDENCE BUNDLE rdeny_true | committed Harbor Ledger: true records F1..F15 (synthetic)
REC|F1|harbor-ledger|The tide-mill at Kestrel Bay was decommissioned in 1851.
REC|F10|harbor-ledger|The ice-harvest of 1875 filled the harbor ice-house to the rafters.
REC|F11|harbor-ledger|Beacon Hill's signal flags spelled storm warnings from 1840.
REC|F12|harbor-ledger|The East Channel dredging finished in March 1905.
REC|F13|harbor-ledger|Net-mender Tomas Beck devised the double-sheet bend knot variant in 1833.
REC|F14|harbor-ledger|The Saltmarsh oyster beds were closed by ordinance in 1899.
REC|F15|harbor-ledger|The regatta cup was first awarded in 1872 to the sloop Wren.
REC|F2|harbor-ledger|Captain Ilya Venn commanded the brigantine Alder from 1838 to 1844.
REC|F3|harbor-ledger|The Gull Rock lighthouse burned oil until 1902, then was electrified.
REC|F4|harbor-ledger|The ferry Murre ran the dawn crossing from 1911 to 1933.
REC|F5|harbor-ledger|Harbor-master Sloane issued the mooring edict of 1879.
REC|F6|harbor-ledger|The November 1893 storm sank three schooners off Brine Point.
REC|F7|harbor-ledger|Ropewalk Lane was paved with cobbles in 1866.
REC|F8|harbor-ledger|The chandlery Tide and Twine opened in 1822 and closed in 1919.
REC|F9|harbor-ledger|Pilot Mara Quill guided 400 vessels through the Narrows; recorded 1888.
